CHAPTER V — BATTLEFIELD SYSTEMS
Obsidian Protocol’s battlefield is not static — it is a living, reactive environment. Terrain, visibility, elevation, weather, suppression, and hazards all interact with autonomous squads, creating emergent tactical behavior without micromanagement.

This chapter defines every environmental system that influences combat, movement, detection, and intent resolution.

5.1 Fog‑of‑War
Fog‑of‑war is dynamic, shaped by sensors, elevation, weather, and unit type.

Fog‑of‑War Layers
Hard Fog — completely unknown; no data

Soft Fog — last known positions; outdated intel

Sensor Fog — visible only through sensors (LIDAR, thermal, recon drones)

Command Fog — visible only within command radius

Fog‑of‑War Influences
Elevation

Weather

Time of day

Sensor type

Unit stealth

Command radius

Environmental hazards

Fog‑of‑war is recalculated continuously and directly affects autonomous decision‑making.

5.2 Visibility Cones
Every unit has a directional visibility cone that determines what it can see.

Cone Properties
Angle — wider for recon, narrower for heavy armor

Range — affected by elevation, weather, and sensors

Height — air units have downward cones; ground units have horizontal cones

Occlusion — blocked by terrain, buildings, smoke, and hazards

Cone Behaviors
Units will:

Reposition to maintain sightlines

Avoid blind spots

Use elevation to extend visibility

Coordinate cones with allied units

Visibility cones are essential for autonomous flanking, breaching, and suppression avoidance.

5.3 Elevation
Elevation is one of the most influential battlefield systems.

Elevation Effects
Increased range

Increased accuracy

Increased visibility

Reduced vulnerability

Improved sensor coverage

Elevation Types
High ground

Low ground

Ridges

Cliffs

Rooftops

Hills

Multi‑level structures

AI Elevation Behavior
Squads will:

Seek high ground

Anchor firing lines on elevated positions

Avoid low‑ground kill zones

Use elevation to flank or breach

Elevation is a core part of autonomous tactical reasoning.

5.4 Cover & Destructible Cover
Cover is dynamic and can be destroyed by weapons, hazards, or environmental effects.

Cover Types
Soft Cover — vegetation, light debris

Hard Cover — walls, vehicles, fortifications

Temporary Cover — smoke, holographic barriers

Destructible Cover — buildings, barricades, cars

Cover Effects
Reduced incoming damage

Reduced suppression

Reduced visibility to enemies

Increased survival during breaches

Destruction Rules
Cover can be destroyed by:

Explosives

Heavy weapons

Experimental units

Environmental hazards

Chain reactions (fire, collapse)

AI dynamically adapts to cover destruction.

5.5 Suppression Zones
Suppression zones represent areas of intense fire that restrict movement and reduce effectiveness.

Suppression Effects
Reduced accuracy

Reduced movement speed

Increased retreat likelihood

Increased cover‑seeking behavior

Increased flanking behavior

Suppression Sources
Machine guns

Artillery

Airstrikes

Naval guns

Experimental weapons

AI Suppression Response
Squads will:

Path around suppression zones

Deploy smoke

Call for support

Flank instead of assault

Retreat if overwhelmed

Suppression is a core part of autonomous decision‑making.

5.6 Fire Lanes
Fire lanes represent optimal shooting corridors.

Fire Lane Properties
Clear line‑of‑sight

Minimal occlusion

High elevation advantage

Overlapping visibility cones

Safe distance from suppression zones

AI Fire Lane Behavior
Squads will:

Anchor firing lines

Maintain overwatch

Cover breach teams

Coordinate multi‑angle assaults

Use fire lanes to suppress enemies

Fire lanes are essential for coordinated autonomous assaults.

